      "claim": "A planet is a celestial body that orbits a star (or the Sun).",
      "evidence": "IAU 2006 definition of a planet requires it to be in orbit around the Sun (for Solar System bodies) and the general definition of exoplanets is a body orbiting a star.",
      "weight": 0.35,
      "depends_on": []
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "claim": "The Sun is the only star in the Solar System.",
      "evidence": "Observations show the Sun contains >99 % of the Solar System's mass and is the central gravitational anchor for all planets.",
      "weight": 0.30,
      "depends_on": []
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "claim": "All planets in the Solar System orbit the Sun.",
      "evidence": "Combining the definition that planets orbit a star (Step 1) with the fact that the Sun is the Solar System's star (Step 2).",
      "weight": 0.20,
      "depends_on": [1, 2]
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "claim": "The Moon orbits Earth, not the Sun directly.",
      "evidence": "Lunar orbital mechanics: the Moon is a natural satellite whose primary gravitational binding is to Earth.",
      "weight": 0.05,
      "depends_on": [1]
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "claim": "Earth is itself a planet, so it cannot be the central body that other planets orbit.",
      "evidence": "Logical classification: a planet cannot serve as the primary gravitating body for other planets in the standard Solar System model.",
      "weight": 0.05,
      "depends_on": [1]
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "claim": "Galaxies are collections of stars; planets orbit their host star, not the galaxy as a whole.",
      "evidence": "Galactic dynamics: stars (and their planets) orbit the galactic centre, but the primary gravitational binding of a planet is to its host star.",
      "weight": 0.05,
      "depends_on": [1]
    }
